{"name":"Black Pearl","hex":"#1e272c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"pearl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shelly concretion, usually rounded, and having a brilliant luster, with varying tints, found in the mantle, or between the mantle and shell, of certain bivalve mollusks, especially in the pearl oysters and river mussels, and sometimes in certain univalves. It is usually due to a secretion of shelly substance around some irritating foreign particle. Its substance is the same as nacre, or mother-of-pearl. Round lustrous pearls are used in jewellery.\nNacre; mother-of-pearl.\n A white color inspired by the shade of pearls.","Nacre; mother-of-pearl."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set or adorn with pearls, or with mother-of-pearl.","To cause to resemble pearls in shape; to make into small round grains."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ennerdale black pearl","blocks":[{"text":"The Ennerdale black pearl is a rare black pearl formed in freshwater pearl mussels found on stretches of the Ehen and Irt rivers in the English county of Cumbria."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ennerdale_black_pearl","wikidata":"Q125881228"},{"title":"Tahitian pearl","blocks":[{"text":"The Tahitian pearl poé poérava is an organic gem formed from the black lip oyster. These pearls derive their name from the fact that they are primarily cultivated around the islands of French Polynesia, around Tahiti."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Perles_de_Tahiti_%283%29.JPG/330px-Perles_de_Tahiti_%283%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahitian_pearl","wikidata":"Q7675555"},{"title":"Black Pearl (Pirates of the Caribbean)","blocks":[{"text":"The Black Pearl is a fictional ship in the  Pirates of the Caribbean film series. In the screenplay, the ship is easily recognized by her distinctive black hull and sails. Captained by Captain Jack Sparrow and Hector Barbossa, the Black Pearl is said to be \"nigh uncatchable\". In the first three films, she either overtakes or flees all other ships, including both the Interceptor, which is regarded as the fastest ship in the Caribbean, and the Flying Dutchman, which is faster than the wind. Her speed is derived from several factors such as the large number of sails she carries and being partly supernatural. As stated in Dead Man's Chest and At World's End, the Black Pearl is \"the only ship that can outrun the Dutchman\" and this is evidenced in the maelstrom battle between the two ships in the movies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/BlackPearl.jpg/330px-BlackPearl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pearl_(Pirates_of_the_Caribbean)","wikidata":"Q19964"},{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Bi_quyet_lua_chon_ngoc_trai_11.png/500px-Bi_quyet_lua_chon_ngoc_trai_11.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Bi_quyet_lua_chon_ngoc_trai_11.png/500px-Bi_quyet_lua_chon_ngoc_trai_11.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tahitian pearl sizes in different colors","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABi_quyet_lua_chon_ngoc_trai_11.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/1280px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Georgian seed pearl gold ring","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/1280px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A black pearl and a shell of the black-lipped pearl oyster. The iridescent colors originate from nacre layers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Nacre_microscopic_structure.png/500px-Nacre_microscopic_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Nacre_microscopic_structure.png/500px-Nacre_microscopic_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of nacre layers, wherein aragonite plates are separated by biopolymers, such as chitin, lustrin and silk-like proteins","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANacre_microscopic_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG/500px-Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG/500px-Bruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Electron microscopy image of a fractured surface of nacre","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABruchfl%C3%A4che_eines_Perlmuttst%C3%BCcks.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-500px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-1280px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf/page1-500px-PearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Formation of non–bead-cultured Akoya \"keshi\" pearls produced in a P. i. fucata mollusk. (A) Optical overview of a nonbeaded keshi–cultured pearl. (B) Cross-section showing CaCO3 growth begins onto an organic center. (C) Mature nacre. (D and E) Atomic-resolution image of atoms in nacre. (F) Transition from spherulitic aragonite structures to nacre. (G and H) Aggregated nanoparticles form massive aragonite. (I) The formation of nacre begins directly on massive aragonite.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APearlCrossSection_Figure1.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg/500px-Two_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two halved river pearls, natural freshwater pearls, on a slab of lapis lazuli. The pearls are about 2.5–3 mm (0.098–0.118 in) in diameter. The growth layers are clearly visible.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo_halved_river_pearls%2C_natural_freshwater_pearls_on_lapis_lazuli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Pearls.jpg/500px-Pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nuclei from Mikimoto Pearl 